Title: THE PRICE OF THE LIVING

[[rdg
Tone: Blood and Thunder
Start: Huang arrives at Haven with twelve bound captives and offers to sell their labour to the struggling colony.
End: Lila agrees to pay and free the captives, but the missing twelfth passenger growls beneath the truck.
]]

[[character: Huang
Gender: Male
Voice: Grandiose, theatrical salesmanship; vivid metaphors, flattery and indignant laughter.
Want: Sell the captives for ammunition, antibiotics and fuel so he can return with more.
Obstacle: Nick challenges his claim to own the people he rescued.
]]

[[character: Overseer Lila
Gender: Female
Voice: Commanding and practical, with forceful imagery and little patience for argument.
Want: Keep Haven alive through winter and bring the captives inside as free people.
Obstacle: Paying Huang may encourage him to bring more captives.
]]

[[character: Dr Nick
Gender: Male
Voice: Dry, precise and cutting; quiet questions give way to anger when a patient is threatened.
Want: Treat the captives and prevent Haven from rewarding slavery.
Obstacle: Haven needs workers and Huang demands payment before releasing them.
]]

[[character: Arif
Gender: Male
Voice: Plain, direct questions that puncture Huang's grand speeches.
Want: Find a place to work as a free man.
Obstacle: Huang has bound him and intends to sell him despite promising freedom.
]]

[[character: Guard
Gender: Male
Voice: Brief, urgent warnings.
Want: Warn Huang about the danger beneath the truck.
Obstacle: The negotiation distracts everyone from the missing captive.
]]

EXT. HAVEN – MAIN GATE – DAY

Beyond Haven's FORTIFIED GATES, a highway littered with abandoned cars and skeletons.

A TRUCK approaches under a white flag. Twelve CAPTIVES sit in the back, wrists bound. Four ARMED GUARDS escort it.

HUANG (50s), immaculate linen shirt, STEPS out of the truck. He looks more like a PROUD FATHER than a SLAVE TRADER.

OVERSEER LILA watches from the parapet. Beside her stands DR NICK, his WHITE COAT stained with IODINE, a rifle slung over his shoulder.

HUANG
Ho, Haven! Open your eyes and behold what fortune has dragged from the jaws of hell! Twelve souls! Twelve stout hearts snatched from the teeth of the dead, and every one worth his weight in gold!

OVERSEER LILA
Fortune comes to our gates in strange garments. Yesterday she wore the rags of a starving child. Today she arrives in a truck with a gun at every corner and twelve prisoners tied like pigs for slaughter.

HUANG
Pigs! Ah, madam, had I brought you pigs, you would have greeted me with flowers! A pig may fill your belly for a week. These men and women may fill your granaries for twenty years!

DR NICK
A generous calculation. Particularly for those who have not yet been asked how long they wish to remain.

HUANG
Doctor! I know a doctor when I see one. That noble stoop! Those hollow eyes! The look of a man who has spent his life dragging fools from the grave only to watch them leap back into it! Come down, sir! I have brought you a treasure!

DR NICK
Treasures are generally buried, Mr Huang. Yours appear to have been dug up.

Huang laughed and STRUCK the side of his truck.

HUANG
Hear him! A physician with the tongue of a serpent! I like a man whose wit has survived the ruin of the world. Come, I shall show you wonders!

Lila DESCENDED with Nick. FOUR GUARDS followed, their rifles ready.

OVERSEER LILA
The gates remain shut. Speak your business beneath the sun, trader, where every man may see your hands.

HUANG
Gladly! I have hidden nothing from honest eyes, save my purse from thieves and my supper from hungry dogs!

He beckoned a GREY-HAIRED WOMAN forward.

HUANG
Behold Farah! Sixty-two winters have passed over that noble head, and not one has bent it! For twenty-three years she fed the children of a school in Klang. When the dead came pouring through the streets like black water through a broken dam, she barred the kitchen doors and fed forty children for nine days! Nine days, with rice enough for three!

OVERSEER LILA
And what became of the children?

HUANG
Twenty-seven escaped. Thirteen perished when the soldiers fired upon the school.

DR NICK
And Farah?

HUANG
She lived! There stands the proof! I found her in a drainage ditch, her feet torn to ribbons, clutching a cooking pot as another woman might clutch her firstborn. I carried her myself for half a mile while the dead snapped at our heels!

DR NICK
You carried her?

HUANG
Upon these shoulders!

DR NICK
Then your shoulders have performed a noble deed. It is unfortunate your hands have since found a rope.

HUANG
Ha! A rope! Would you have me lead twelve strangers through a land of wolves with nothing but a smile and a prayer? The world is no longer a garden, Doctor! It is a butcher's yard, and every man who walks abroad must choose whether he shall wield the knife or lie upon the block!

DR NICK
There are men who have made that choice without troubling themselves to ask the cattle.

HUANG
You speak of cattle. I speak of Farah! Look upon her hands! They have fed more children than all the fine gentlemen who sat in Parliament and promised that no child should hunger!

OVERSEER LILA
Then why does she stand before me bound?

HUANG
Because the living are worth guarding!

OVERSEER LILA
A strange guardianship, that fastens a chain upon the neck of its beloved.

HUANG
And what of your walls, Overseer? Are they not chains of stone? Do you not lock your gates against the night? Every mother who bars her door against wolves is a jailer, if one follows your philosophy into its dark little hole!

OVERSEER LILA
My people hold the keys to their own doors.

HUANG
And when the dead come, do you let them open those doors?

Lila regarded him for a moment.

OVERSEER LILA
Show me the others.

Huang spread his arms triumphantly.

HUANG
Gladly! Arif! Come forth, you magnificent son of a thunderbolt!

A THIN MAN climbed down from the truck.

HUANG
This fellow was an electrician when electricity still flowed from the walls like wine from a king's cellar! I saw him bring a dead generator roaring back to life with wire stolen from a washing machine and a copper ring taken from his own wedding finger!

DR NICK
A wedding ring?

HUANG
His wife's! He wore it upon a chain about his neck. She had died in the first winter.

DR NICK
And you took the ring?

HUANG
He gave it freely! I asked him what use a ring might serve when his friends lay freezing, and he broke the chain himself! Ah, Doctor, you should have seen that machine awaken! Its lamps blazed like a hundred little suns, and the children shouted as though dawn had come twice in one day!

DR NICK
Then he has already given you something precious.

HUANG
Indeed! And he shall give you still more! A man who can coax fire from dead iron is worth five hundred rounds of ammunition, and I would call any man a fool who offered less!

ARIF
You promised me a place where I could work.

HUANG
And here it stands! A kingdom behind walls! Fields! Workshops! A doctor! Have I not kept my word?

ARIF
You promised I would be free.

HUANG
Free! A word men fling about like a copper coin, though none can agree what it buys! You were free when I found you, Arif. Free to starve! Free to freeze! Free to become supper for the first corpse that caught your scent!

DR NICK
And now he is free to be sold.

HUANG
Now he is free to live!

DR NICK
A remarkable freedom. It appears to belong entirely to you.

Huang wheeled upon the doctor.

HUANG
Tell me, Doctor! How many men have you saved beyond these walls? How many miles have you marched with the dead upon your heels? How many nights have you slept in a ditch with one eye open and a knife beneath your hand, while a dozen frightened souls huddled about you like children beneath a mother's skirts?

DR NICK
Fewer than you, I imagine.

HUANG
Ah! At last, an honest word!

DR NICK
I have spent most of my time among the sick. There are so many of them. They arrive with fever in their blood and wounds full of corruption, and I have learned that death is a greedy beast. One may snatch a man from its jaws today and find it waiting beneath his bed tomorrow.

HUANG
Then we are brothers in battle!

DR NICK
Perhaps. Though I have never thought to present my patients with a bill for their lives.

HUANG
You dwell behind walls! Your medicine grows in cupboards! Your water springs from pipes! I have wrestled with death upon the open road, and you condemn me because I demand bread for the mouths I have fed!

DR NICK
Bread would be a modest demand. You ask for rifles, bullets, medicine, and the right to bring another truck.

HUANG
Aye! That I may save another twelve! And twelve after those! Shall I leave them to the wolves because the good Doctor finds my methods distasteful?

Lila raised a HAND.

OVERSEER LILA
Enough! Your quarrel has grown fat while my people grow thin. We have fields lying fallow, pumps that cough mud, and a fence whose timbers are rotten as the teeth of the dead. Twelve willing hands might save this colony before winter buries us beneath its white shroud.

DR NICK
And twelve unwilling hands?

OVERSEER LILA
Shall be given the choice to work or walk away!

HUANG
A noble promise! And one I shall be delighted to see you fulfil, once the price is paid.

DR NICK
There lies the serpent in the grass.

HUANG
Serpent! I have been called a vulture, a jackal, a carrion crow! Now I am a serpent! By heaven, Doctor, you shall have me transformed into the entire animal kingdom before our bargain is done!

DR NICK
And still you would find a price for every beast.

Huang threw back his head and laughed.

HUANG
I would! For the vulture cleans the field, the jackal hunts the rat, and the serpent kills the vermin that would eat your grain! The world has use for every creature, Doctor, even those whose faces offend the delicate!

OVERSEER LILA
What is your price for all twelve?

HUANG
Three thousand rounds. Two crates of antibiotics. Fuel enough to carry my men south.

Lila's face darkened.

OVERSEER LILA
Three thousand! You would strip the teeth from our walls and leave us grinning at the dead!

HUANG
Would you have twelve strong backs for the price of a sack of potatoes? Look upon them! Not one is a drunkard, a thief, or a coward! I have watched them through fire and famine! I know their virtues as a father knows his children!

DR NICK
Then name their birthdays.

Huang PAUSED.

HUANG
Their birthdays?

DR NICK
You know their virtues. Their courage. Their sufferings. You speak of them as a father. Tell me when they were born.

HUANG
What devil's game is this?

DR NICK
No game. A birthday is a small thing. A cake, perhaps. A candle. A child waiting to be told he has grown another year older. Surely a father remembers.

HUANG
Doctor, you would have me count candles while the dead devour the earth!

DR NICK
No. I would have you remember that there were candles before there were chains.

For the first time Huang's face showed FURY.

HUANG
You dare lecture me upon humanity? You, who sit in safety while I drag its broken remnants from the wilderness? I have seen a mother cut the flesh from her own dead husband to feed her children! I have seen brothers cast lots to decide which should be left behind! I have walked through towns where the living nailed their doors shut from within and waited for hunger to finish what the dead had begun!

He SEIZED Arif by the shoulders.

HUANG
This man would be bones beneath a highway bridge had I not found him! Farah would be a skull grinning from a ditch! Mei Ling would be wandering the roads with a fever in her blood and the dead at her heels! I have bought their food with my own blood, guarded their sleep with my own eyes, and buried men who died to keep them breathing! And you, Doctor, who have sacrificed nothing for them, would rob me of the means to save another twelve!

DR NICK
You have sacrificed much.

HUANG
Aye!

DR NICK
And I fear you intend to make the world repay you until its last living soul has passed through your hands.

Huang released Arif.

HUANG
Then let the world repay me! Let it give me gold, bullets, kingdoms! I shall take them all and spend them upon the living! What would you have me do with my riches? Build a palace? Crown myself emperor of the ashes?

DR NICK
You already have a kingdom, Mr Huang. It rides in the back of your truck.

Silence.

From the truck came the sound of a WOMAN COUGHING.

OVERSEER LILA
The woman is ill.

HUANG
Mei Ling. A nurse of great skill. She has tended my men through wounds that would have killed an ox.

DR NICK
Bring her down.

HUANG
Six hundred rounds.

DR NICK
Bring her down, or I shall climb that truck and carry her myself!

Huang GLARED at him.

HUANG
Ah! The physician bares his fangs at last!

DR NICK
I have buried children this month, Mr Huang. I have washed their blood from my hands and returned to find their mothers waiting with other children in their arms. My patience is a poor, threadbare garment, and you have been tugging at it since noon!

HUANG
Then buy her!

DR NICK
I will treat her!

HUANG
And when she is well?

DR NICK
She may spit in my face and walk away, if that is her pleasure!

HUANG
A fine reward for saving her life!

DR NICK
It would be the finest I have ever received!

Lila STEPPED BETWEEN THEM.

OVERSEER LILA
Silence, both of you! Shall a woman perish while two proud men wrestle over the ownership of her breath?

She turned to Huang.

OVERSEER LILA
You shall have your ammunition.

DR NICK
Lila!

OVERSEER LILA
But hear me, trader! The instant these twelve pass beneath my gate, every rope shall fall and every debt shall die. They shall stand upon Haven's earth as free men and women. If they wish to work, I shall pay them. If they wish to leave, I shall open the gate. And if you return with another truck of captives, I shall offer them the same mercy!

Huang's eyes shone.

HUANG
Then we understand one another! You have a heart fit for a queen, Overseer!

DR NICK
And he shall return with twenty-four next time. Forty-eight after that. A hundred men dragged from their homes to feed the hunger of our compassion!

OVERSEER LILA
Shall I condemn these twelve to spare those I have never seen?

DR NICK
Shall we purchase twelve lives with the lives of a hundred more?

HUANG
Ha! You would have them die upon the road to preserve the purity of your conscience! A noble funeral! I shall carve upon their graves: *Here lie twelve souls whom a good man would not buy!*

Nick's hand closed upon the strap of his RIFLE.

DR NICK
Take care, Mr Huang.

HUANG
Of what? Your gun? I have stood before a thousand dead men and heard their teeth clatter like hail upon a roof! Shall I tremble because one living man has grown angry?

DR NICK
No. You should tremble because you have mistaken a man's restraint for cowardice.

Lila DREW her pistol and FIRED once into the earth between them.

OVERSEER LILA
By all the graves beyond these walls, I have had enough!

The report ROLLED across the abandoned highway. Far away, something answered with a LONG, HUNGRY CRY.

OVERSEER LILA
There! Hear them? The dead have no quarrel with slavery, no opinion upon mercy, no patience for our splendid arguments! They shall come whether we buy these people or bury them! And when they come, I shall need hands upon the walls, rifles upon the towers, and men and women who believe this miserable patch of earth is worth dying for!

She pointed at Huang.

OVERSEER LILA
You shall be paid for bringing them alive.

She pointed at Nick.

OVERSEER LILA
You shall heal them, and you shall have your freedom to curse me for the bargain!

Then she turned to the captives.

OVERSEER LILA
And you! Hear me well! No man shall own you within these walls. But neither shall you find a paradise! You shall find hunger, labour, cold nights, and death waiting beyond the wire! Choose to enter, and you shall bear these burdens beside us, as free people!

Arif STEPPED FORWARD.

ARIF
And if we refuse?

OVERSEER LILA
Then you may go where you please, and may fortune guard your steps!

Huang smiled.

HUANG
A splendid speech! Worthy of the old kings! Now, Overseer, shall we count the ammunition?

DR NICK
One question first.

HUANG
Another! By heaven, Doctor, your questions are more numerous than the dead!

DR NICK
You spoke of twelve people. Yet I count eleven in the truck.

Huang's smile vanished.

Lila turned toward the vehicle. ELEVEN FACES looked back.

OVERSEER LILA
Where is the twelfth?

HUANG
He was there when we left the river.

DR NICK
Then where is he now?

From beneath the truck came a dull metallic KNOCK.

One of Huang's guards backed away.

GUARD
Boss—

Another knock. Then a low, wet GROWL.

HUANG
Ah.

He DREW his PISTOL.

HUANG
That, my friends, is a matter upon which I fear we must renegotiate.
